CHUNK({"chunk":"M","module":"m4","topics":[{"id":"m4-water-act-basics","title":"Water Act, 1974: background and boards","short":"Water Act basics","blurb":"Why the Water Act came, its constitutional basis under Article 252, the 1988 amendment, the seven needs it meets, and the CPCB, SPCBs and Joint Boards it created.","source":"Lecture W4 (Water Act, Part II), pages 1–3","tabs":[{"title":"Background","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974</b>: India's <b>first comprehensive water pollution law</b>, <b>23 March 1974</b>. Aim: maintain/restore <b>wholesomeness</b> of water.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Why: Ganga, Yamuna, Godavari, Cauvery hit by industry, sewage, farm runoff.</li>\n            <li><b>Article 252</b>: Parliament legislates on a <b>state subject</b> (water) at states' request.</li>\n            <li><b>1988 amendment</b>: higher penalties, wider Board powers.</li>\n            <li>The Act has <b>64 sections in 10 chapters</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">The Water Act <b>created the CPCB and SPCBs</b>; they later took on Air Act duties too. It is the origin of India's environmental regulatory set-up.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Seven needs","html":"\n          <ol>\n            <li>Protection of public health</li>\n            <li>Preservation of aquatic life</li>\n            <li>Sustainable water for future generations</li>\n            <li>Economic considerations</li>\n            <li>Uniform national standards</li>\n            <li>Scientific and technical expertise</li>\n            <li>International obligations</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Boards","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>CPCB, Section 3</b></td><td>National: clean streams/wells; advises Centre; coordinates SPCBs; training; research; information; national water standards</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SPCB, Section 4</b></td><td>State: advises State Govt; programmes; inspects sewage/effluent plants; training; consent, monitoring, labs, prosecution</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Joint Board, Section 13</b></td><td>Two or more states (inter-state rivers)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>Functions: <b>Section 16</b> (Central Board: advisory, coordination, research), <b>Section 17</b> (State Board: inspection, monitoring, enforcement), <b>Section 18</b> (both Boards give <b>binding directions</b>).</p>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Date the Water Act was enacted?","back":"<b>23 March 1974</b>."},{"front":"Constitutional basis of the Water Act?","back":"<b>Article 252</b>: Parliament legislates on a state subject at states' request."},{"front":"Which law created the CPCB and SPCBs?","back":"The <b>Water Act, 1974</b>."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-water-act-provisions","title":"Water Act: key provisions","short":"Water Act provisions","blurb":"Sections 24, 25 and 26 on discharge, sections 21 and 23 on sampling and entry, consent and MINAS, emergency powers, and appeals to the NGT.","source":"Lecture W4 (Water Act, Part II), pages 3–5, 9–10","tabs":[{"title":"Discharge","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 24</b></td><td><b>Absolute prohibition</b>: no poisonous, noxious or polluting matter into a <b>stream or well</b> without consent</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 25</b></td><td><b>Consent for new outlets</b> (like Air Act S21); approve, reject or conditions</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 26</b></td><td><b>Existing discharges</b>: seek consent by deadline</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 27</b></td><td>Revocation of consent</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Consent conditions: effluent <b>quality, quantity, monitoring</b>; depend on receiving water's capacity. <b>MINAS</b> = Minimum National Standards for effluent, set by CPCB; states may be stricter.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Inspection and emergencies","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 21</b></td><td><b>Effluent samples</b> (notify, recognised labs, legal evidence)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 23</b></td><td><b>Entry and inspection</b>: plants, records, consent compliance</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 32</b></td><td><b>Emergency measures</b>: remove pollutants, restore water after accidents</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 33</b></td><td>Boards may apply to <b>courts</b> to stop apprehended pollution</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 28</b></td><td>Appeal to the State Government against Board decisions</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 29</b></td><td>State Government can revise Board orders</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Section 33B</b></td><td>Appeal to the <b>National Green Tribunal</b> (set up 2010, fast-track environmental court)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Water Act section with the absolute ban on polluting streams and wells?","back":"<b>Section 24</b>."},{"front":"Water Act section for consent for new outlets?","back":"<b>Section 25</b>."},{"front":"Section 26 covers…","back":"<b>Existing</b> discharges, which must seek consent by a deadline."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-water-act-penalties","title":"Water Act penalties and the three Acts compared","short":"Water Act penalties","blurb":"Penalty sections 41 to 47, key features of offences, why engineers must know the Act, and a quick reference for EPA, Air Act and Water Act.","source":"Lecture W4 (Water Act, Part II), pages 5–10","tabs":[{"title":"Penalties","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Violation</th><th>Penalty</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>41</td><td>Not following Section 20 (information) or Board directions</td><td>1.5–6 years + fine</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>41A</td><td>Not following Section 32 or court orders (33/33A)</td><td>1.5–6 years + fine</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>42</td><td>Obstructing officers, procedural violations</td><td>Up to 3 months or ₹10,000 or both</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>43</b></td><td>Breaking <b>Section 24</b> (stream/well)</td><td>1.5–6 years + fine (<b>most severe</b>)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>44</td><td>Breaking Section 25/26 (no consent)</td><td>1.5–6 years + fine</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>45</td><td>Continuing after conviction</td><td>Up to <b>₹5,000 per day</b> extra</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>46</td><td>Offences by <b>companies</b></td><td>Directors, managers, engineers in charge liable unless due diligence shown</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>47</td><td>Offences by <b>government departments</b></td><td>Head of Department liable</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Non-compoundable</b>: no settling by fine; court mandatory.</li>\n            <li>Some <b>cognizable</b>: arrest without warrant.</li>\n            <li>Penalties go to the <b>Environmental Protection Fund</b>.</li>\n            <li>Offenders remain liable for <b>cleanup and restoration</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "},{"title":"Why engineers","html":"\n          <p>Ten reasons: ethics; compliance (S25 consent, S20 records, S23 inspections); <b>personal liability, Section 46</b>; careers (consulting, compliance, Boards, audits); sustainable design (<b>zero liquid discharge</b>, reuse); regulators; local water; research; governance; avoid delays, penalties, shutdowns.</p>\n          <p>First-year steps: Sections 24–27, 41–45; CPCB effluent standards; plant visits; CPCB/SPCB sites; case studies; records.</p>\n        "},{"title":"Three Acts compared","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Water Act 1974</th><th>Air Act 1981</th><th>EPA 1986</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Amended</td><td>1988 (penalties, Board powers)</td><td>1987 (noise added)</td><td>Enacted 19 Nov 1986 after Bhopal</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Key sections</td><td>24 ban, 25 consent, 27 revocation, 32 emergency</td><td>19 control areas, 21 consent, 24 samples, 31A closure</td><td>3 powers, 6 standards, 7 ban, 8 hazardous, 10 entry</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Penalty</td><td>1.5–6 years + fine</td><td>Up to 3 months + ₹10,000</td><td>Up to 5 years + ₹1 lakh (7 years if continued)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\">The Air Act penalty here is the course's figure for basic violations. Learn the numbers exactly as given in this table.</div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Water Act imprisonment range for serious violations?","back":"<b>1.5 to 6 years</b> plus fine."},{"front":"Section for continuing violation fines?","back":"<b>Section 45</b>: up to <b>₹5,000 per day</b>."},{"front":"Most severe penalty section?","back":"<b>Section 43</b>, for breaking Section 24."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[]});
